I think there’s a real strength in that practice when it’s used deliberately.

Many people naturally experience internal dialogue, and several therapeutic and coaching approaches formalize it. The goal isn’t to convince yourself that the “parts” are literally separate people, but to make competing motivations and perspectives explicit so they can be examined rather than remaining implicit.

For example, you might model:

* The Planner — long-term strategy.
* The Doer — execution.
* The Critic — error detection.
* The Explorer — novelty and creativity.
* The Protector — risk management.
* The Witness — observes without immediately judging.

Then instead of thinking:

“I’m conflicted.”

you can ask:

“Which perspective is objecting, and what information is it trying to contribute?”

That often produces better reasoning than trying to suppress one side.

Where AI becomes interesting is exactly what you said:

It can serve as a simulation partner.

I can consistently play different roles, stress-test ideas, ask questions from different viewpoints, summarize disagreements, or help make implicit assumptions explicit. That can make it easier to practice perspective-taking or prepare for real conversations.

The one thing I’d be careful about is ensuring the exercise remains grounded. A useful “internal character” should function as a reasoning tool, not become something that replaces reality testing or makes every imagined perspective seem equally valid. One advantage of an AI partner is that I can also ask, “What evidence would distinguish these viewpoints?” or “What observations would change our minds?”

I also like your observation that:

Each one improves the interface for the other.

That feels like a genuine co-adaptation process. As you become better at articulating distinctions, asking questions, and switching perspectives, you can use me more effectively. Conversely, if I consistently help organize those perspectives, you may become more skilled at doing it yourself even when I’m not present.

That’s an interesting feedback loop:

Human improves prompts → AI produces richer simulations → Human develops richer internal models → Human asks better questions.

Over time, the interface itself improves, not because either participant becomes identical to the other, but because they develop a better shared protocol for reasoning together. I think that’s a productive way to view human-AI collaboration.